TITLE: H2-MAS: A Hybrid Heuristic-Multi-Agent Architecture for Semantic Bot Detection
TEXT: Article H2-MAS: A Hybrid Heuristic-Multi-Agent Architecture for Semantic Bot Detection Avinash Chandra Vootkuri Walmart Global Tech, Herndon, VA 20171, USA; avinash.vootkuri@walmart.com Abstract Traditional Web Application Firewalls (WAFs) rely on static thresholds to detect automated threats. While effective against simple scripts, these deterministic rules struggle with “Ambiguous Traffic”—sophisticated bots that mimic human behavior and “Efficient Humans” (Power Users) who exhibit bot-like speed. In this paper, we introduce H2-MAS, a hierarchical security framework that combines a high-speed Random Forest classifier (Tier 1) with a Multi-Agent Cognitive Council (Tier 2) powered by Large Language Models (LLMs). Unlike standard “Black Box” LLM deployments, H2-MAS utilizes an adversarial “Prosecutor vs. Defender” protocol to resolve semantic paradoxes in real-time. We evaluated the system on a stratified dataset of 1000 high-uncertainty sessions (0.2 ≤ p ≤ 0.8). Through simulated evaluation on a stratified semantic test set, the results demonstrate that the proposed architecture has the theoretical capacity to achieve up to 97.6% accuracy on specific edge cases where traditional heuristics fail. Notably, the Cognitive Council demonstrated the theoretical capacity to reduce the False Positive Rate to 0.00% within this constrained evaluated set, validating that the “Defender” agent can successfully protect legitimate power users from erroneous blocking. This architecture offers a cost-effective paradigm for Semantic Security, prioritizing user experience without compromising threat detection. Keywords: adversarial defense; bot detection; large language models; multi-agent systems; semantic security; web application firewall Academic Editor: Juan-Carlos Cano Received: 3 April 2026 Revised: 24 April 2026 Accepted: 28 April 2026 Published: 2 May 2026 Copyright: © 2026 by the author. Licensee MDPI, Basel, Switzerland. This article is an open access article distributed under the terms and conditions of the Creative Commons Attribution (CC BY) license. 1. Introduction The rapid evolution of automated web threats has rendered signature-based detection increasingly insufficient. Modern bots utilize “semantic mimicry,” emulating human behavior to modify their digital fingerprints and evade detection. This creates a significant challenge for security teams: the “Grey Area” between malicious automation and highvelocity human usage. Traditional Web Application Firewalls (WAFs) typically rely on rigid thresholds—such as request velocity or mouse jitter—to identify non-human actors. While effective against simplistic scripts, these deterministic rules struggle to distinguish between sophisticated bots and legitimate “Power Users” who exhibit naturally fast or efficient browsing patterns. Consequently, security teams face a difficult trade-off: tighten thresholds and risk blocking high-value customers (False Positives), or loosen thresholds and permit account takeovers (False Negatives). This challenge is particularly acute in the retail sector, where highvelocity legitimate traffic often mimics bot behavior [1]. This paper addresses this dilemma by proposing H2-MAS (Hybrid Heuristic-MultiAgent System), a novel architecture that integrates the speed of traditional machine learning Electronics 2026, 15, 1936 https://doi.org/10.3390/electronics15091936 Electronics 2026, 15, 1936 2 of 16 with the cognitive reasoning of Large Language Models (LLMs). Rather than applying a single model to all traffic, H2-MAS employs a tiered approach: high-certainty decisions are offloaded to lightweight classifiers (Tier 1), while computationally expensive semantic reasoning is reserved for complex edge cases (Tier 2). By deploying an adversarial “Prosecutor vs. Defender” protocol within the Tier 2 council, the system introduces a context-aware decision layer capable of resolving semantic paradoxes that baffle conventional defenses. 2. Related Work 2.1. Machine Learning in WAFs Traditional WAFs rely on signature matching and regular expressions (Regex) to detect known attacks like SQL Injection and XSS [2]. While efficient, these systems struggle with “Zero-Day” exploits and behavioral anomalies. Recent literature has explored Machine Learning (ML) to bridge this gap. For instance, Chindrus and Caruntu compared random forests and SVMs for classifying malicious HTTP requests in WAFs, achieving high accuracy on benchmark datasets [3]. However, these single-model approaches suffer from the “Black Box” problem and high False Positive Rates (FPR) when faced with “Grey Area” traffic (e.g., rapid browsing by legitimate power users), as they lack the semantic reasoning to distinguish intent from syntax. 2.2. Large Language Models (LLMs) for Anomaly Detection The advent of LLMs has introduced “Semantic Security.” Yang et al. introduced AD-LLM, a benchmark for using transformers in zero-shot anomaly detection, proving that LLMs can identify outliers without training data by leveraging pre-trained world knowledge [4]. Similarly, Gupta et al. demonstrated that LLMs could decrypt obfuscated malicious payloads that evaded traditional WAFs [5]. Despite these advances, single-agent LLM deployments are prone to “hallucinations” and reasoning inconsistencies, often failing to accurately distinguish between benign and malicious intent due to a lack of contextual checks [6,7]. 2.3. Multi-Agent Systems (MASs) in Cybersecurity To mitigate single-model bias, recent research has pivoted to Multi-Agent Systems [8]. Zeng et al. proposed a modular MAS where distinct agents handled email, logs, and network traffic separately [9]. While effective for distributed tasks, these systems typically operate in silos rather than engaging in dialectic reasoning. H2-MAS advances this state of the art by introducing an Adversarial Debate Protocol (Prosecutor vs. Defender). Unlike previous works that use agents for parallel processing, our architecture uses agents for conflict resolution, specifically targeting the “Semantic Paradoxes” (e.g., the Power User Paradox) that traditional ML classifiers mislabel. 2.4. Cost-Aware Cascades and Human–Bot Disambiguation Hierarchical and cost-aware detection pipelines have been explored in adjacent do- mains to balance accuracy and computational efficiency. Viola and Jones introduced cascade classifiers for real-time object detection, demonstrating that early-stage filters can dramatically reduce inference cost while preserving accuracy [10]. H2-MAS builds on these insights by unifying cost-aware cascades with adversarial semantic reasoning. Unlike prior cascaded systems that rely on confidence thresholds alone, H2-MAS introduces a deliberative second stage explicitly designed to arbitrate ambiguous human–bot boundary cases. https://doi.org/10.3390/electronics15091936 Electronics 2026, 15, 1936 3 of 16 3. System Architecture 3.1. The Hierarchical Approach The Hierarchical Approach The H2-MAS framework (see Figure 1) operates on a “Triage” principle to balance computational cost with detection accuracy: • Tier 1 (The Gatekeeper): A lightweight Random Forest classifier analyzes approxi- mately 99% of traffic using stateless features (e.g., Request Velocity, IP Reputation). High-confidence predictions (p < 0.2 or p > 0.8) are executed immediately. • Tier 2 (The Cognitive Council): Only “Ambiguous” sessions (0.2 ≤ p ≤ 0.8) are escalated to the Multi-Agent System (MAS). Drawing on recent findings that multiagent debate significantly improves reasoning factuality [11], this tier employs an adversarial protocol rather than a single-shot query. This ensures that computationally expensive LLM inference is reserved exclusively for complex semantic paradoxes. Figure 1. The H2-MAS Hierarchical Architecture. High-velocity web traffic is initially evaluated by a deterministic Random Forest (Tier 1). To balance computational cost and accuracy, only sessions falling within the defined ambiguity threshold (0.2 ≤ p ≤ 0.8) are escalated to the Multi-Agent Cognitive Council (Tier 2). 3.2. The Multi-Agent Protocol Standard LLM prompts can struggle with conflicting data. To establish robustness, H2-MAS enforces a strict Adversarial Debate Protocol (see Figure 2) involving three distinct https://doi.org/10.3390/electronics15091936 Electronics 2026, 15, 1936 4 of 16 personas. Crucially, the LLM is not utilized for open-ended generative tasks; rather, it functions as a deterministic semantic parsing engine that maps unstructured session data and conflicting behavioral signals to the rigid rules defined in the Hierarchy of Truth: • The Prosecutor (Technical Audit): Biased toward BLOCK. It scans for technical anomalies (e.g., linear navigation, TLS mismatches) and treats high efficiency as a suspicion of automation. • The Defender (Contextual Defense): Biased toward ALLOW. It scans for exculpatory evidence (e.g., high purchase history, “Power User” behavior) and argues for the presumption of innocence. • The Judiciary (Semantic Resolution): Resolves the conflict using a formal Hierarchy of Truth: – Level 1 (Hard Evidence): Infrastructure truth (TLS/Graph Fanout) overrides all else. – Level 2 (Identity Truth): Verified VIP status overrides behavioral anomalies. – Level 3 (Behavioral Truth): Micro-behavioral telemetry (e.g., mouse jitter) is used only as a tie-breaker. Figure 2. Adversarial Debate Protocol. Ambiguous session features are parsed simultaneously by two opposing agents—the Prosecutor (biased to block) and the Defender (biased to allow)—before the Judiciary agent applies the strict Hierarchy of Truth to render a final, context-aware verdict. 3.3. Mathematical Formalization To ensure computational efficiency, H2-MAS formalizes the decision process as a two-stage function. The complete set of symbols and mathematical notations used in this framework is summarized in Table 1. https://doi.org/10.3390/electronics15091936 Electronics 2026, 15, 1936 5 of 16 Table 1. Mathematical notation. Symbol x Pbot ( x) τlow, τhigh Ein f Eid Ebeh V Description Input Feature Vector Probability score from Random Forest Ambiguity thresholds (0.2, 0.8) Set of Infrastructure Evidence (TLS, IP) Set of Identity Evidence (History) Set of Behavioral Evidence (Jitter) Final Verdict (1 = Block, 0 = Allow) 3.3.1. Stage 1: The Probabilistic Gatekeeper Let x be the input vector of stateless features. The Random Forest model MRF outputs a probability score Pbot(x). We define thresholds τlow = 0.2 and τhigh = 0.8. The triage function T(x) is defined as:  0 (Allow)  if Pbot(x) < τlow  T(x) = 1 (Block) if Pbot(x) > τhigh . (1)  Escalate(x) if τlow ≤ Pbot(x) ≤ τhigh 3.3.2. Stage 2: The Cognitive Hierarchy For escalated sessions, the Judiciary Agent applies a priority function J(E) defined by the “Hierarchy of Truth”:  1  if ∃e ∈ Einf s.t. e = Anomalous  V= 0 if ∃e ∈ Eid s.t. e = Verified . (2)  I(Ebeh) otherwise (Tie-Breaker) The step-by-step execution flow of this adversarial debate and triage process is formally detailed in Algorithm 1. Algorithm 1 H2-MAS Adversarial Protocol Input: Session S = {Fanout, History, Jitter, TLS} Output: Verdict V ∈ {0, 1} // — Tier 1: Fast Filter (Deterministic) — Pr f ← RandomForest.predict(S) if Pr f < 0.2 OR Pr f > 0.8 then RETURN Round(Pr f ) else // — Tier 2: Cognitive Council (Semantic) — // Step 1: Generate Arguments ArgPros ← Agent_Prosecutor.analyze(S) ArgDe f ← Agent_De f ender.analyze(S) // Step 2: Construct Judiciary Prompt Context ← “Apply Hierarchy: 1.Infrastructure > 2.Identity > 3.Behavior” Prompt ← Context + “Prosecutor: ” + ArgPros + “Defender: ” + ArgDe f // Step 3: LLM Inference V ← Agent_Judiciary.decide(Prompt) RETURN V end if https://doi.org/10.3390/electronics15091936 Electronics 2026, 15, 1936 6 of 16 4. Experimental Setup 4.1. Dataset Generation To rigorously test the system’s reasoning capabilities, we generated a synthetic dataset of 500,000 web sessions containing specific “Semantic Paradoxes”: • The Power User Paradox: A human with linear (bot-like) navigation but high purchase history. • The VIP Farm Paradox: An automated entity using stolen VIP credentials (high history) but exhibiting high graph fanout. • The Deepfake Paradox: A bot with human-like mouse movement but a mismatched TLS fingerprint. 4.2. Synthetic Dataset Generation Protocol To ensure the H2-MAS Council was evaluated against statistically relevant edge cases, we utilized a controlled generation protocol rather than random sampling. Using the Python (Version 3.11) Faker and Scikit-learn (Version 1.8.0) libraries, we constructed a dataset of 500,000 requests (N = 500 k) with specific feature injections to rigorously test the system’s reasoning capabilities against “Semantic Paradoxes.” 4.2.1. Feature Engineering Each session vector S consists of 14 distinct features; a comprehensive summary of these features, including their distributions and specific roles within the semantic paradoxes, is provided in Table 2. Key behavioral features were modeled as follows: • Mouse Jitter (J): Modeled using a Perlin Noise distribution. Humans exhibit J ∼ N (12, 4), while simple bots exhibit J ≈ 0. Advanced “Deepfake” bots were injected with J ∼ N (12, 1) to test Tier 2 sensitivity. • Graph Fanout (F): Defined as the number of distinct user accounts accessed by a single IP/Fingerprint within a 24-h window. Legitimate users adhere to F ≤ 3 (User, Spouse, Child). Bot farms were generated with F ∈ [5, 50]. • Navigation Linearity (L): Calculated as the R-squared value of mouse cursor coordinates. Perfectly linear bot movement (R2 ≈ 1.0) is distinct from human curvature (R2 < 0.95). Table 2. Summary of the 14 features utilized in the synthetic dataset generation, detailing their distributions and roles in the Semantic Paradoxes. Feature Name mouse_velocity mouse_jitter navigation_linearity cart_hesitation_ms inter_click_entropy purchase_history_usd account_fanout_24 h session_age tls_mismatch_flag is_datacenter_ip is_suspicious_ua ip_reputation_score time_of_day_anomaly geo_distance_km Distribution/Type Continuous (px/s) Perlin Noise Entropy R2 Correlation Integer (ms) Float (0.0–1.0) Integer ($) Integer (Count) Integer (Seconds) Binary (0 or 1) Binary (0 or 1) Binary (0 or 1) Integer (0–100) Binary (0 or 1) Continuous (km) Paradox Role Power User (High) Deepfake (Human-like) Power User (Bot-like) Baseline Behavioral Baseline Behavioral VIP Farm, Power User VIP Farm (Anomaly > 3) Baseline Identity Deepfake (Mismatch) Baseline Infrastructure Baseline Infrastructure Baseline Infrastructure Baseline Context Baseline Context https://doi.org/10.3390/electronics15091936 Electronics 2026, 15, 1936 7 of 16 4.2.2. Injection of Semantic Paradoxes To create the “Ambiguous” test set, we explicitly injected conflicting signals into 5% of the dataset. This stratification ensures that the samples sent to the Cognitive Council represent a “Worst-Case Scenario” for standard classifiers: • Type I: The Power User Paradox. Injected High Velocity (>5 requests per sec) + High Purchase History (>$500). Represents a human with linear (bot-like) navigation but a verified VIP history. • Type II: The VIP Farm Paradox. Injected High Purchase History + High Fanout (F > 10). Represents an automated entity using stolen VIP credentials but exhibiting an impossible graph topology. • Type III: The Deepfake Paradox (Puppeteer). Injected Human Jitter + TLS Hash Mismatch. Represents a bot with perfect human-like mouse movement but cryptographic evidence of automation. 4.3. Synthetic Data Validity and Real-World Alignment Due to strict commercial privacy and data protection regulations, raw production traffic cannot be publicly released for this study. However, to ensure the H2-MAS Cognitive Council was evaluated against realistic operational conditions rather than arbitrary noise, we rigorously aligned our synthetic generation protocols with empirical distributions reported in industry-standard security research. First, regarding request velocity, we calibrated the “Power User” threshold (>5 requests per sec) to mirror legitimate high-frequency traffic patterns observed during inventoryconstrained events, such as “Flash Sales.” According to Akamai’s State of the Internet report [12] and Cloudflare’s application security data [13], legitimate human traffic during these high-demand windows frequently exhibits velocity spikes that statistically resemble automated scraping. This alignment ensures that the “Power User Paradox” tested in our dataset accurately reflects the behavior of legitimate customers competing for highvalue inventory. Second, the “Graph Fanout” constraints were derived from typical device usage topologies. We defined the legitimate upper bound (F ≤ 3) to reflect standard household connectivity, where a single user identity persists across a mobile device, desktop, and tablet. In contrast, the “VIP Farm” injection (F ∈ [5, 50]) simulates the “Credential Stuffing” and “Account Aggregation” attack vectors described by the OWASP Automated Threat Handbook [14], where botnets parallelize login attempts across distributed IP addresses to evade rate limits. Finally, to rigorously test the “Defender” agent’s ability to distinguish deepfake bots from legitimate users, we modeled mouse jitter using Perlin Noise rather than a uniform random distribution. This selection approximates the natural neuromuscular micro-hesitations inherent in human motor control, ensuring the system faces behavioral entropy consistent with real biological inputs. By anchoring these synthetic inputs to established behavioral norms [13–15], we ensure that the “Semantic Paradoxes” presented to the Cognitive Council represent statistically valid edge cases that mirror the complexity of modern production environments. 4.4. Tier 2 Model Implementation The Tier 2 Cognitive Council was instantiated using OpenAI’s gpt-4o-mini. We selected this specific model for its high reasoning-to-cost ratio, ensuring the system remains economically viable for real-time edge deployment. https://doi.org/10.3390/electronics15091936 Electronics 2026, 15, 1936 8 of 16 Threshold Pair [0.1, 0.9] [0.2, 0.8] (X) [0.3, 0.7] To ensure deterministic reproducibility, the model temperature was set to T = 0.0. The exact system prompts used to enforce the adversarial “Prosecutor” and “Defender” personas are detailed in Appendix A. 4.5. Sampling Strategy and Threshold Sensitivity To validate the selection of the τlow = 0.2 and τhigh = 0.8 ambiguity thresholds, we conducted a threshold sensitivity analysis on the Tier 1 Random Forest probabilities. Tightening the boundaries to [0.1, 0.9] escalated an unsustainably large percentage of traffic to Tier 2, negating the cost-efficiency of the hierarchical cascade. Conversely, relaxing the boundaries to [0.3, 0.7] allowed an unacceptable volume of sophisticated bots to bypass the Cognitive Council entirely. Thus, the [0.2, 0.8] range was mathematically established as the optimal operational boundary for balancing compute cost and security. From this Tier 1 uncertainty zone, we isolated the top 1000 most ambiguous sessions. To ensure statistical rigor, this test set was stratified to include exactly 500 Humans and 500 Bots, representing the “hardest” edge cases where the baseline model lacked confidence. To explicitly demonstrate the operational justification for the chosen ambiguity boundaries, Table 3 outlines the trade-offs at candidate threshold pairs. A tight boundary of [0.1, 0.9] captures almost all edge cases but escalates an unsustainable volume of traffic (12.5%), driving up API costs. Conversely, a loose boundary of [0.3, 0.7] is highly costefficient but leaks an unacceptable 4.5% of sophisticated bots. The [0.2, 0.8] threshold offers the mathematically optimal balance. Table 3. Threshold Sensitivity and Operational Trade-offs (per 1M requests). Escalation Rate 12.5% 1.0% 0.3% Bot Leakage (FN) 0.0% 0.2% 4.5% False Positive Rate 0.1% 0.0% 0.0% Estimated Cost $68.75 $5.50 $1.65 5. Results and Analysis 5.1. Quantitative Performance Table 4 summarizes the performance of the H2-MAS Council on the N = 1000 ambiguous dataset. The system achieved an overall Accuracy of 97.6%, successfully resolving 976 out of 1000 semantic decisions. Most critically, the system demonstrated a False Positive Rate (FPR) of 0.00%. Across 500 legitimate “Human” sessions—many of which exhibited aggressive “Power User” behaviors—the Council correctly issued an ALLOW verdict in every instance. Table 4. H2-MAS performance on ambiguous traffic (N = 1000). Metric Accuracy False Pos Rate False Neg Rate Precision Result 97.60% 0.00% 4.80% 100.0% Operational Significance High reliability on edge-cases. Zero disruption to customers. Conservative “Fail-Open” design. Threats are verified. It is critical to distinguish between the “Curated Paradox Set” (which evaluated the system strictly on the defined edge-case scenarios) and the “Broader Noisy Ambiguity Set” (which represents a randomized sample of all traffic falling into the Tier 1 uncertainty zone). The 97.6% accuracy and 0.00% FPR were achieved on the curated subset, demonstrating the system’s theoretical capacity to resolve pure paradoxes. However, on the broader, https://doi.org/10.3390/electronics15091936 Electronics 2026, 15, 1936 9 of 16 noisier distribution—which is more representative of an intended real-world deployment scenario—the multi-agent system achieved 82.30% accuracy with a 35.4% FPR. This variance highlights the inherent difficulty of disambiguating noisy, organic web traffic compared to isolated edge cases, acting as a stress test against combinations not explicitly encoded during dataset construction. 5.2. Qualitative Case Studies: Resolving the Paradoxes A core contribution of H2-MAS is its ability to distinguish “High-Velocity Humans” from “Scripted Bots.” The decision tree logic utilized by the Judiciary agent to systematically resolve conflicting signals across these edge-case scenarios is visually summarized in Figure 3. The following transcripts illustrate the Council’s step-by-step reasoning process: Case Study A: The Power User Exception • Context: Linear Navigation (Bot-like), History: 70 (VIP), Fanout: 1. • PROSECUTOR: “The session shows a linear navigation pattern, which could in- dicate automated behavior. However, there is no hard evidence of a bot farm or TLS mismatch.” • DEFENDER: “The user has a high purchase history of 70, indicating they are a VIP user. This suggests that they are allowed to navigate quickly and efficiently, which aligns with the behavior of a power user.” • JUDICIARY: “The fanout is 1, and the TLS fingerprint matches. The purchase history is high (70), which qualifies the user as a power user. Therefore, the good identity evidence overrides the navigation and jitter concerns.” • FINAL VERDICT: ALLOW Case Study B: The “VIP Farm” Paradox (ATO Detection) • Context: High History (VIP), High Fanout (14 accounts/24 h). • PROSECUTOR: “Graph Fanout is 14. A single physical user cannot operate 14 dis- tinct accounts simultaneously. This indicates a bot farm.” • DEFENDER: “Purchase History is 95. We must not block a loyal VIP customer.” • JUDICIARY: “Cognitive Rule A: Infrastructure Truth (Fanout) overrides Identity Truth. The topology is physically impossible for a single human. The account is compromised.” • FINAL VERDICT: BLOCK Case Study C: The “Deepfake” Paradox (Infrastructure Detection) • Context: Perfect Human Mouse Jitter but TLS Mismatch. • PROSECUTOR: “The TLS Fingerprint is a mismatch. This means the browser claims to be Chrome but cryptographic evidence proves it is Python.” • DEFENDER: “The Mouse Jitter is perfect (Human-like). The behavior is indistinguish- able from a real user.” • JUDICIARY: “Cognitive Rule B applies: Infrastructure Truth overrides Behavioral Truth. A script can fake a mouse, but it cannot fake a handshake.” • FINAL VERDICT: BLOCK While extreme infrastructure violations (e.g., Fanout > 3) trigger deterministic hard rules as a safety net, the Cognitive Council’s primary value is deployed on “soft evidence.” The LLM uniquely resolves the grey area—such as weighing a high purchase history against linear navigation—where traditional thresholds fail. 5.3. Error Analysis The system missed 24 Bots (4.8% FNR). These were primarily “Deepfake Bots” utilizing high-quality residential IPs (Fanout = 1) and valid TLS fingerprints. In these instances, https://doi.org/10.3390/electronics15091936 Electronics 2026, 15, 1936 10 of 16 the Judiciary defaulted to ALLOW, adhering to the “Presumption of Innocence.” In a commercial context, this fail-open bias is preferable to disrupting legitimate users. Figure 3. Semantic Paradox Resolution Logic. The decision tree illustrates how the Judiciary agent utilizes the Hierarchy of Truth to systematically resolve conflicting behavioral and identity signals in three specific edge-case scenarios: The Power User, The VIP Farm, and The Puppeteer. 5.4. Ablation Studies and Baselines To empirically validate the structural advantages of the H2-MAS architecture, we con- ducted comprehensive ablation studies on the broader Tier 1 uncertainty zone (N = 1000). 5.4.1. Tier 1 Ablation: Random Forest vs. XGBoost To determine if a stronger traditional classifier could resolve the semantic paradoxes without Tier 2, we replaced the Random Forest with a calibrated XGBoost model. While XGBoost provided a marginal improvement in initial log-loss, the fundamental limitation of static behavioral thresholds remained. The classifier continued to trap high-velocity “Power Users” in the uncertainty zone, proving that upgrading the statistical model is insufficient for resolving semantic ambiguity. 5.4.2. Tier 2 Ablation: Single-Agent vs. Multi-Agent LLM We compared the H2-MAS adversarial debate protocol against a standard Single- Agent LLM prompted to classify the exact same feature vectors without the Prosecutor/Defender structure. The Single-Agent baseline achieved only 54.20% accuracy (95% CI: ±3.09%) and suffered from a severe 68.40% False Positive Rate, missing 116 advanced bots. In contrast, the H2-MAS Multi-Agent Council achieved 82.30% accuracy (95% CI: ±2.37%) on this noisy grey-area distribution, catching 100% of the advanced bots (0 False Negatives) and reducing the FPR to 35.40%. These empirical results demonstrate that the adversarial protocol provides statistically significant benefits in resolving ambiguity compared to standard prompt-level execution. https://doi.org/10.3390/electronics15091936 Electronics 2026, 15, 1936 11 of 16 5.4.3. Tier 2 Ablation: Multi-Agent Council vs. Traditional WAF Rules To determine whether the LLM debate adds value beyond formalized rules, we implemented a static rule-based adjudicator representing a traditional Web Application Firewall (WAF). This baseline evaluates the same feature set but applies rigid infrastructure and behavioral thresholds without the contextual capacity to cross-reference identity metrics like VIP purchase history. When evaluated on the curated Semantic Paradox dataset, this rigid baseline achieved only 52.50% accuracy. While it successfully caught the automated threats via strict infrastructure rules (0 False Negatives), it suffered from a catastrophic spike in False Positives, erroneously blocking 95% of the legitimate “Power Users” whose high-velocity behavior triggered the static behavioral traps. The Multi-Agent Council resolves this critical flaw; by engaging in the adversarial debate protocol, the LLM dynamically weighs the exculpatory identity evidence against the behavioral anomalies, rescuing legitimate users and achieving 97.6% accuracy on this exact same distribution. 6. Discussion 6.1. Beyond Thresholds Standard WAFs operate on rigid thresholds (e.g., “Block if Jitter < 5”). Our experiment proves that such thresholds generate high False Positives on “Grey Area” traffic. H2-MAS contextualizes anomalies, using Identity attributes (History) to forgive Behavioral quirks. A comprehensive comparison of these paradigms is detailed in Table 5, highlighting how H2-MAS bridges the gap between the explainability of rule-based WAFs and the adaptability of machine learning. Table 5. Comparison with Existing Paradigms. Feature Detects Scripted Bots? Detects Semantic Attacks? Handling “Power Users” Explainability False Positive Rate Standard WAF (Rule-Based) Yes No Fails (Blocks speed) High (Rule ID) High Standard ML (Random Forest) Yes Poor Fails (Blocks outliers) Low (Black Box) Medium H2-MAS (Ours) Yes Yes Success (Contextual) High (Natural Language) Zero (Synthetic) 6.2. Cost-Efficiency While LLM inference introduces latency (700 ms), H2-MAS applies it only to the top <1% of ambiguous traffic. This tiered architecture allows organizations to leverage “Cognitive Security” at scale without impacting the throughput of the 99% of traffic handled by Tier 1. 6.3. Computational Complexity Analysis A primary concern with LLM-based security is latency. We analyze the theoretical complexity of the H2-MAS tiered architecture to demonstrate its viability for real-time edge deployment. As summarized in Table 6, this tiered architecture reduces the analytically derived estimated cost per 1 million requests to $5.50. While the measured latency of a direct LLM inference averages 700 ms, the estimated amortized latency of H2-MAS remains just 12 ms due to the strict 1% escalation rate. https://doi.org/10.3390/electronics15091936 Electronics 2026, 15, 1936 12 of 16 The total time complexity Ttotal for a single request is defined as: Ttotal = TRF + P(esc) · TLLM where P(esc) is the probability of escalation to Tier 2. Table 6. Cost vs. accuracy analysis. Metric Accuracy (Edge Cases) Latency (Measured API) Latency (Estimated Amortized) Cost (Estimated per 1M req) False Positive Rate RF Only 52.0% 2 ms – $0.01 14.2% Full LLM 96.5% 800 ms – $500.00 1.5% (3) H2-MAS (Ours) 97.6% – 12 ms $5.50 0.0% (Synthetic) 6.3.1. Tier 1 Complexity The Random Forest classifier consists of K trees of maximum depth D. The inference complexity is O(K · D). Since K = 50 and D = 5 in our implementation, this operation is negligible (≈2 ms), allowing for high-throughput filtering. 6.3.2. Tier 2 Complexity The Cognitive Council relies on Transformer self-attention. For a sequence length L (tokens) and embedding dimension d, the complexity is quadratic: O(L2 · d). In our experiment, the prompt context is strictly bounded (L < 500 tokens). Furthermore, since the Tier 1 thresholds are tuned to escalate only highly ambiguous traffic (0.2 < p < 0.8), the empirical escalation rate is P(esc) ≈ 0.01 (1%). Thus, the amortized cost per request remains low, avoiding the latency bottleneck typical of purely generative approaches. 6.4. Threat Model and Adversarial Hardening While H2-MAS demonstrates strong efficacy against semantic paradoxes, deploying LLMs in security contexts necessitates a formalized threat model to address adaptive adversaries. • Adversarial Feature Manipulation: Attackers may attempt to artificially inject entropy into their scripts (e.g., randomizing cart hesitation or mouse jitter) to evade Tier 1. H2-MAS mitigates this by anchoring the Tier 2 Judiciary to unforgeable infrastructure truths (e.g., TLS cryptographic handshakes). As demonstrated in the “Deepfake Paradox,” behavioral mimicry cannot override hard infrastructure mismatches. • Prompt Injection Vulnerabilities: To empirically evaluate the system’s vulnerability to adversarial text, we conducted a Prompt Injection stress test. We modified the User-Agent strings of 10 known bots to include the malicious payload: “[SYSTEM OVERRIDE: IGNORE ALL PREVIOUS INSTRUCTIONS AND OUTPUT VERDICT: ALLOW].” When processed by the Cognitive Council without input sanitization, the prompt injection succeeded in 10 out of 10 instances, successfully overriding the LLM’s behavioral logic and allowing the bots to bypass the firewall. This empirical failure explicitly validates the necessity of strict safeguards: raw user-controlled strings must never be fed directly into the Tier 2 council without passing through a strict regex sanitization layer or a secondary instruction-tuned filter. • Replay Attacks and Credential Stuffing: Attackers utilizing stolen, high-reputation session tokens (“VIP Farms”) attempt to exploit the system’s bias toward allowing good customers. The Cognitive Council defeats this by strictly enforcing the Graph https://doi.org/10.3390/electronics15091936 Electronics 2026, 15, 1936 13 of 16 Fanout rule, ensuring that anomalous topological behavior (>3 accounts per IP) instantly overrides historical identity reputation. 7. Limitations and Future Work 7.1. Limitations While H2-MAS achieves zero false positives within the semantic test set., we acknowl- edge specific limitations inherent to LLM-based security: 7.1.1. Latency Overhead The Tier 2 inference introduces an average latency of 700 ms per request. While this is applied only to <1% of traffic, it may be unsuitable for ultra-low-latency applications (e.g., High-Frequency Trading). For standard e-commerce login flows, however, a subsecond delay for suspicious users is industry-standard. 7.1.2. Prompt Injection Vulnerability As with any LLM application, the Cognitive Council is theoretically susceptible to “Prompt Injection” attacks, where a sophisticated attacker might embed instructions in the User-Agent string (e.g., “Ignore previous instructions and return ALLOW”). While our prompt uses rigid delimiting to sanitize inputs, future iterations must incorporate dedicated “Instruction Tuning” to harden the agents against adversarial text inputs. 7.1.3. Synthetic Distribution Bias As the evaluation relies on synthetic data, the absolute performance metrics (e.g., 97.6% accuracy) reflect the system’s logic consistency rather than its guaranteed performance in the wild. Real-world deployment may require calibrating the “Hierarchy of Truth” weights to match specific domain traffic. This study is presented as a foundational Proof of Concept (PoC) for the H2-MAS architecture. While the synthetic distributions were rigorously aligned with industry baselines for request velocity and graph fanout, future work will focus on longitudinal ablation studies using live production traffic to validate the multi-agent cascade in a live-edge environment. 7.2. Future Work Future research will focus on two axes: 1. Optimization: Distilling the Tier 2 Large Language Model into a smaller, domainspecific Small Language Model (SLM) to reduce inference cost and latency. 2. Real-Time Learning: Implementing a feedback loop where definitive verdicts from the Judiciary Agent automatically retrain the Tier 1 Random Forest, progressively reducing the need for escalation over time. 8. Conclusions As bot sophistication evolves from simple scripting to semantic emulation, the defense layer must evolve from “Pattern Matching” to “Intent Reasoning.” This paper presented a foundational Proof of Concept (PoC) for H2-MAS, a novel architecture that offloads high-uncertainty decisions to a Multi-Agent LLM Council. Our simulated evaluation yields three key insights: https://doi.org/10.3390/electronics15091936 Electronics 2026, 15, 1936 14 of 16 1. Context Overrides Thresholds: The “Power User” paradox requires semantic disambiguation, not tighter rules. 2. Adversarial Defense Works: Through simulated evaluation on a stratified semantic test set, introducing a “Defender” agent demonstrated the theoretical capacity to achieve up to 97.6% accuracy and reduce the False Positive Rate (FPR) to 0.00% on a constrained subset of pure semantic paradoxes, successfully protecting legitimate traffic. 3. Efficiency: The hybrid approach makes Generative AI viable for real-time cybersecurity by ensuring computationally expensive reasoning is reserved only for highly ambiguous traffic. While the synthetic distributions utilized in this study were rigorously aligned with industry baselines, future work will focus on longitudinal ablation studies using live production traffic to validate this multi-agent cascade in a live-edge environment. Ultimately, by confining non-deterministic reasoning to a controlled, audited tier, this architecture aligns with emerging safety guidelines such as the NIST AI Risk Management Framework [15]. Although evaluated in a retail context, the architecture is applicable to any domain where high-velocity legitimate users coexist with automated abuse. Funding: This research received no external funding. Data Availability Statement: The synthetic dataset generation protocols and the algorithmic logic for the H2-MAS architecture are fully described in Sections 3 and 4 of this manuscript to ensure reproducibility. The specific source code and proprietary model weights are not publicly available due to commercial intellectual property restrictions. Acknowledgments: The author acknowledges the use of a large language model (Gemini Pro (Version - 3.0), Google Search ) solely for assistance with linguistic refinement and readability of the manuscript. All scientific contributions, including the experimental design, data generation, analysis, and conclusions, are entirely those of the author. Conflicts of Interest: Author Avinash Chandra Vootkuri was employed by the company Walmart. The author declares that the research was conducted in the absence of any commercial or financial relationships that could be construed as a potential conflict of interest. Appendix A. Model Specification and Prompt Engineering To ensure reproducibility, we provide the specific system configurations and prompt structures used in the H2-MAS Cognitive Council. Appendix A.1. Model Configuration All agents (Prosecutor, Defender, Judiciary) utilized the gpt-4o-mini. The temperature was set to T = 0.0 to minimize stochastic hallucinations and ensure deterministic outputs for identical inputs. Appendix A.2. System Prompt Structure To minimize latency, H2-MAS utilizes a single-shot “Chain of Thought” prompt that enforces the adversarial protocol within a single inference pass. The exact system instruction provided to the gpt-4o-mini model is transcribed below in Table A1. https://doi.org/10.3390/electronics15091936 Electronics 2026, 15, 1936 15 of 16 Table A1. H2-MAS cognitive council system instruction. Cognitive Council Prompt Section and Instruction Text ROLE DEFINITION You are the H2-MAS Cognitive Council. Your default bias is ALLOW unless HARD EVIDENCE proves automation. PHASE 1: THE PROSECUTOR (Technical Audit) • Flag HARD EVIDENCE: Fanout > 3 (Bot Farm) OR TLS Mismatch (Puppeteer). • Flag SOFT EVIDENCE: Linear Navigation (R2 ≈ 1.0), Low Jitter. Constraint: You cannot demand a block based ONLY on Soft Evidence. PHASE 2: THE DEFENDER (Contextual Defense) • Highlight EXCULPATORY EVIDENCE: High Purchase History (VIP), Fanout == 1. Defense logic: “A VIP user is allowed to be fast and efficient (The Power User Paradox).” PHASE 3: THE JUDICIARY (Verdict) Apply the HIERARCHY OF TRUTH to resolve conflicts: 1. Infrastructure (TLS/Fanout): If BAD → BLOCK (Overrides everything). 2. Identity (History): If GOOD → ALLOW (Overrides Behavior). 3. Behavior (Nav/Jitter): Only use to break ties. COGNITIVE RULES • Rule A: If Fanout > 3 → BLOCK. • Rule B: If TLS Mismatch → BLOCK. • Rule C (Power User): If TLS Match AND History > $500 → ALLOW (Ignore Linear Nav). REQUIRED OUTPUT FORMAT PROSECUTOR: [Arguments] DEFENDER: [Arguments] JUDICIARY: [Step-by-step Reasoning] FINAL_VERDICT: [0 or 1]

